\details{
\subsection{Why this function is useful}{

\code{\link[gstat:krigeST]{gstat::krigeST()}} with local neighborhood selection requires two key
hyperparameters: \code{nmax} (the number of neighbors used for each prediction)
and \code{bufferNmax} (a multiplier controlling the size of the candidate pool
from which those neighbors are selected). While \code{nmax} can be chosen based
on domain knowledge and cross-validation without too much trouble, \code{bufferNmax} is harder to tune
because its role in algorithm is not obvious without understanding the
internal mechanics of \code{\link[gstat:krigeST]{gstat::krigeST()}}. Even then --- guessing an informative
range of values to start your search is nearly impossible unless you have extensive experience
with modeling data from that domain (k's upper bound is number of dates × number of locations).

Internally, \code{krigeST.local} uses \code{\link[FNN:get.knnx]{FNN::get.knnx()}} to find
\code{k = ceiling(bufferNmax * nmax)} nearest neighbors in the \strong{combined}
spatio-temporal metric space (where time is rescaled by \code{stAni}). It then
evaluates the actual covariance between all \code{k} candidates and the
prediction location, keeping only the \code{nmax} with the strongest covariance.
If \code{bufferNmax} is too small, the candidate pool may not contain the truly
most correlated observations leading to suboptimal predictions.

The \code{nmax} value should be obtained via cross-validation. Just use a 1, 2,
or 3 for \code{bufferNmax} and select the best \code{nmax} according to which model has
the lowest SSE. Then use that \code{nmax} value in this function.

This function provides a principled estimate for \code{bufferNmax} so that an
empirical grid search can start from a informative value which allows for a
more efficient search.
}

\subsection{How the function works}{

The function estimates \code{bufferNmax} in three steps:

\strong{Step 1: Compute the effective range of the joint variogram component.}
The effective range is defined as the distance at which the correlation of
the joint variogram component drops to \code{cor_level}. For a Matérn model this
is computed numerically using \code{\link[stats:uniroot]{stats::uniroot()}} since there is no closed
form solution for all values of kappa. For a Spherical model the effective
range equals the range parameter exactly since the variogram reaches its
sill at that distance by definition. For an Exponential model the effective
range is also computed numerically. A \code{cor_level} of 0.01 (the default) is
used rather than the conventional 0.05 because \code{bufferNmax} needs to cast a
wide enough candidate net to capture all observations with any meaningful
covariance — not just those within the conventional effective range.

\strong{Step 2: Count spatio-temporal observations within the effective range.}
For a random sample of \code{samp_size} spatio-temporal observation locations,
the function computes the joint metric distance from each sampled location
to all other observations using:
\deqn{d_{ST} = \sqrt{d_S^2 + (d_T \times stAni)^2}}
where \eqn{d_S} is the spatial distance in kilometers and \eqn{d_T} is the
temporal distance in days scaled by \code{stAni} to convert to kilometers. The
number of observations within \code{joint_eff_range} is counted for each sampled
location and averaged across the sample.

\strong{Step 3: Derive \code{bufferNmax}.}
The average neighborhood size \code{k_est} is divided by \code{nmax} to give the
\code{bufferNmax} estimate. This result should be treated as a guess --- the true optimal
\code{bufferNmax} should be confirmed via a cross-validation grid search starting
with this estimate at the center of the grid.
}
